; C14  Winning 42 pp. 93-96 (W42-p093-c, W42-p096-a; Hands 26 and 27)
; Rule, in our words: when an opponent turns out to hold trumps the bidder
; cannot pull, the bidder stops leading trump and cashes a non-trump double,
; the one whose suit has the most tiles still unplayed, while the trump holder
; must still follow.
; Python gate (pour_bidder gate_c14): bidder on lead, exactly one trump trick
; so far, led by the bidder, in which exactly one opponent followed; trump
; still out; the book's tiles are the non-trump doubles whose suit has the
; most unplayed tiles outside the hand.
; How this encoding differs: it has neither the trigger nor the "most
; unplayed": it leads any non-trump double while a trump is out. That is a
; much wider rule, written down only to name what is missing. The book's own
; crisis (Hand 26) comes after two trump tricks, where the gate does not fire;
; in Hand 31 (p. 106) the book's crisis lead is an off, not a double.
; GAP declarer: the bidder is written in as S0.
; GAP history: "one trump trick so far, led by the bidder" reads earlier
; tricks; a policy sees only the current trick.
; GAP shown-void: "exactly one opponent followed it" is one opponent shown
; void in trump and the other not.
; GAP aggregate: "the suit with the most unplayed tiles" compares the sizes of
; two sets of tiles.
(policy c14-crisis-cash-the-double
  (initial play)
  (fallback lowest-legal)
  (rule cash-a-double (in play) (next play) (select answer)
    (guard
      (query
        (roles (domino doubled) (domino out))
        (out doubled)
        (pattern
          (viewer S0)
          (leader S0)
          (next-actor S0)
          ; GAP history, GAP shown-void: the trigger
          (live out)
          (in out q*)
          (not (own-legal out))
          (own-legal doubled)
          (double doubled)
          (not (in doubled q*))
          ; GAP aggregate: and no double of mine has more of its suit unplayed
          )))))
